WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
Florida Exchange Program Day 7

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.250
<i>I wanna keep this up!</i>

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.230
<i>I don't want to change my plan, movements, 
mindset, or anything if I don't have to!</i>

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.470
<i>What's most important now isn't 
doing my best so I can win.</i>

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.030
Game: Maruo. 5-all.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.940
<i>It's doing what I've always done before:</i>

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.790
<i>believing in myself!</i>

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.330
Game: Maruo. 6-5.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:24.420
Maruo leads.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.920
Game, set.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.450
Winner: Maruo. Score: 7-5.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.410
<i>Finally...</i>

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.430
<i>I finally won!</i>

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.030
<i>My first win in America!</i>

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.360
<i>Oh, right, before I forget...</i>

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.510
Right before your first point, you fixed 
your strings and took a deep breath.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.330
You played a lot better after that.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.360
Yeah!

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.370
There's no real reason for that, though.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:29.220
After you fell, you instinctively 
went to fix your strings.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.280
It was a task someone as methodical 
as you could do proactively.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.880
You then turned it into a 
logical routine and repeated it.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.660
If you turn a proactive task into a routine,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.390
its effect is that much greater.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.610
Your logic directed

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.430
your superb instinctive 
decision to a positive result.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.130
Your instincts chose the best method to win.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.000
You felt it was okay to believe in yourself, right?

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.770
Yeah!

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.470
<i>Honestly, I was just desperate.</i>

00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.470
Hey, Maruo.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.110
You can finally play against Soji now.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.620
<i>That's right...</i>

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.620
<i>I won a match, so I get to 
play a match against Ike-kun.</i>

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.480
Thanks, Alex.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.340
Believing in myself turned out 
to be really important.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.330
Ike-kun!

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.600
I finally won!

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.370
Oh, cool!

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.640
So, when do you think we can play a match?

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.780
Well... It's too late today,

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.490
so how about tomorrow before breakfast?

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.480
I'm fine with that, of course.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.210
Then I'll ask the coach, just in case.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.640
He'll be at the party tonight, too.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.540
Party?

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.440
Oh? You hadn't heard?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.160
Data for the Future

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.160
Six, five, four,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.210
three, two, one...

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.510
Happy New Year!

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.710
Oh, right...

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.260
It's already New Year's.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.180
Hey, now, did you seriously forget?

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.950
Everyone here goes home for Christmas,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.810
but we celebrate the New Year together here.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.430
Oh...

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.980
Oh, Eichiro!

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.970
Marcia and Alex!

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.950
So you finally won today, huh?

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.330
It's all thanks to me!

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:18.960
What?

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.140
Uh... Thanks, Marcia.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:21.820
Ahem!

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.280
Hey, once you finish that, 
head back and get to bed.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.670
What?!

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.730
But I just got here!

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:32.580
That's not fair! You get to have all the fun!

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.240
Don't argue. Do you realize what time it is?

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.260
Jeez, stop treating me like a kid!

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.960
You already had enough fun, didn't you?

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.440
I get how she feels,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.590
What if you can't get up tomorrow morning?

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.780
but Marcia's still only thirteen.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.970
I can get up just fine!

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:44.970
Thirteen?!

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.200
Which means...

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.330
In Japan, she'd be in her 
first year at middle school?!

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:52.980
What?

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.110
<i>I thought she was around my age.</i>

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.230
What are you staring at?!

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.980
Ow! That hurts!

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.480
You're way too full of yourself, Eichiro!

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.480
That's amazing, So-chan!

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.410
You're totally meant to play tennis!

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.490
You think so?

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.170
But...

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.520
I'm good at baseball and soccer, too.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.500
You should definitely play tennis!

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.580
You'll totally get really good at it, So-chan!

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.880
Shut up!

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.920
I'm a genius at everything!

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.470
But I guess I wouldn't mind getting 
a little more serious about tennis.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:27.860
Yeah!

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:33.890
Sub: Ei-chan
So when Ei-chan gets over there,
 help him out, will you?
I think he'll get a lot better.  
That way, I think you'll get even
 better too, So-chan!

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.850
<i>Natsu hasn't said anyone would 
"get better" since way back then.</i>

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:36.650
Maruo-kun!

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.310
Oh, Ike-kun!

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.650
Our match tomorrow morning was approved.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.090
But there's something else that 
I've been meaning to ask you.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.790
What's going on between you and Natsu?

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.030
G-Going on? Where'd this come from?

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.330
I guess you'd say, uh... W-We're friends.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.660
Oh?

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.540
Speaking of, you've been friends with 
Nat-chan since you were kids, right?

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:03.050
Yeah. I'm also her fiancé.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.550
I was waiting for that response!

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:12.610
Is that true? Or a joke?

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:13.360
Which is it?

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.060
I-It's a joke!

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:15.620
For real?

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.040
S-Sorry, it was a joke!

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.680
The serious ones are scary when they get mad.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.170
It was really a joke?

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.060
I swear it was a joke! C'mon, forgive me!

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.060
Hey, what's everyone so excited about?

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.520
We're talking about Maruo's girlfriend.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.470
What?! Eichiro, you have a girlfriend?!

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:29.340
<i>On that day,</i>

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.380
<i>I got to talk with everyone 
about a lot of things.</i>

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:37.570
<i>We talked about our families, 
significant others, friends...</i>

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.250
<i>And about our favorite tennis athletes.</i>

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.870
<i>We even talked about our futures in tennis.</i>

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.090
<i>In the end, we just talked about tennis.</i>

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.900
<i>And the next morning, the first of the new year,</i>

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:53.600
Florida Exchange Program Day 11
January 1st

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:58.080
<i>my first opponent of the year was on the court.</i>

00:06:58.850 --> 00:06:59.810
Hey.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.100
Good morning.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:03.880
Did you have fun last night?

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:04.860
Yeah.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.430
Despite some setbacks, it looks like
you're having a good time here.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.960
Every day goes by so quickly.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.620
I think this is the first winter break 
I've had that's gone by this fast.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.660
There were times when I was 
too disappointed to have fun.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:22.010
But this was the first time I ever
felt that way because of tennis.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:25.380
All of those experiences together

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.250
made me realize how much I enjoy tennis.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:29.280
I'm glad I got to come here.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.730
That's good.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.020
We don't have much time, 
so let's play one set.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:33.650
All right.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:38.390
<i>From the moment I started tennis, up to now...</i>

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.520
<i>I thought it was fun.</i>

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:44.230
<i>I wanted to keep playing forever, 
so I wanted to go pro.</i>

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.520
<i>But after coming here</i>

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.440
<i>and meeting so many different people,</i>

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.610
<i>I realized it wasn't just fun.</i>

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:51.690
<i>There's still so much I don't know.</i>

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.630
<i>That makes me believe there are other 
joys I've yet to experience, too.</i>

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.780
<i>Ike-kun is the same age as I am</i>

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:01.080
<i>and chose to go pro.</i>

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.900
<i>I wonder what kind of life he has,</i>

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.420
<i>and how he feels about it.</i>

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.440
Hey, Ike-kun.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.680
What's it like being a pro?

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.720
That's a pretty broad question.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.740
Oh, I mean like... tennis, and your life, 
and how you feel about it...

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.810
I thought some of that might change.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:21.810
You want me to tell you?

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.300
Okay. If you beat me.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:26.110
<i>Wha—</i>

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:28.070
<i>How mean can you get?!</i>

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.150
<i>How likely does he think it is that I'll win?!</i>

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:35.990
<i>B-But if I say it's not possible,</i>

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:39.080
<i>it'd be like admitting defeat, 
and I don't want that!</i>

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.100
Okay!

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.250
Let's play.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.080
Okay.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.130
<i>Bring it!</i>

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:49.770
<i>Go in!</i>

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:51.840
<i>It hit the net!</i>

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.840
<i>But Ike-kun will get it.</i>

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:56.090
<i>A lob?</i>

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.420
<i>P-Please reach it!</i>

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:01.590
<i>No, I </i>will<i> reach it!</i>

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.360
All right, 15-love.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.950
<i>He got to my smash and the 
ball that clipped the net,</i>

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.610
<i>and he even won the point!</i>

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.620
<i>Not only did he return both my luckiest 
shot and my best shot on this court,</i>

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:23.160
<i>he also won the point!</i>

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.620
<i>Then... What can I do to win points?</i>

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.790
<i>What kind of shot do I make?</i>

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.530
30-love.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:42.650
Was it out?

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.130
It was in!

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:45.100
40-love.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.860
Game: Ike. 0-1.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.910
<i>How is this game so one-sided, it's scary?</i>

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.240
<i>I thought he was great before, 
but this goes beyond that!</i>

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.120
<i>Maybe it's because he's up
against an amateur like me,</i>

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.740
<i>but he's thorough in his offense 
and never regrets the risks in it.</i>

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:07.250
<i>No matter where I hit it, he'll return 
it for a point in the next instant.</i>

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.140
<i>Where did all my hard work go?</i>

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.250
<i>No, that's not it!</i>

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.670
<i>I can understand this intensity 
because of that hard work!</i>

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.510
Game: Ike. 0-2.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.530
3-0.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.300
<i>Most of my strokes are returned 
and scored with short rallies.</i>

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.270
<i>Ike-kun missed only five 
opportunities to score on me.</i>

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.020
<i>This is the intensity of someone who's 
competing to be the best in the world!</i>

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.610
Game: Ike. 0-4.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:40.780
5-0.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:44.360
<i>I'm getting a glimpse into 
the world of a top-level pro.</i>

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.130
All right, that's 6-0.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:10:59.680
I win!

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.450
D-Damn...

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.770
H-He's too strong.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.650
Hey...

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:17.150
When I asked you what it's like to be a pro,

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:19.510
and you said that you'd tell me if I won,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:22.410
did you mean I'd <i>understand</i> if I won?

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:27.160
You have your own pro world, 
and Alex has his.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.690
And only those who keep winning

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:31.320
and go pro can understand 
what that world is like.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.700
You were telling me to become a pro 
and experience it myself, right?

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.270
Like I said, you have to beat me to find out.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:39.330
See you later!

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.110
<i>I want to get better!</i>

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:58.580
<i>Much better!</i>

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.950
Florida Exchange Program Day 13

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.950
<i>It's already the thirteenth day.</i>

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.200
<i>Tomorrow is the last day of 
my Florida exchange program.</i>

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.970
<i>Today will probably be my 
last practice match here.</i>

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:24.650
Game. Winner: Terry. Score: 7-5, 6-4.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:26.750
Damn it!

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:29.470
The second 4-5 game... That was it!

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:32.980
If I hadn't made such a stupid mistake, 
I could've turned that game around!

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.010
<i>Have I been focusing too much 
on winning since I went pro?</i>

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.450
Thinking about it after losing 
won't change the results.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:45.490
Dad...

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:49.610
No matter how much you regret it, 
you can't go back to that second 4-5 game.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:54.840
I know that already, but still...

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:57.390
What are you panicking for?

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.200
It looks to me like you've felt pressured 
to win since you turned pro.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:04.530
Yeah. That's probably it.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.100
But is that wrong?

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.430
I knew your business wasn't going well.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:15.800
But you didn't say anything, put me 
through school, and even hired me a coach.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.020
Of course.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:21.030
And now you're putting 
Marcia through school, too.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.390
So I did my best to hurry and go pro,

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:29.530
so I could win tournament 
prize money to help you out.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.810
But instead, I just keep losing.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.180
I'm making you suffer even more.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:40.730
Thinking about stuff like that is what 
makes you obsess over the past.

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:42.800
Forget such silly things.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.800
You prevailed, didn't you?

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:48.700
What is there in your past for you to regret?

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:52.310
Stop looking back, believe in 
yourself, and face forward!

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:55.910
Alex!

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.830
Why don't you play Maruo today?

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:00.850
<i>With Alex?</i>

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:04.270
<i>I'd appreciate it, but he rejected me</i>

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:07.530
<i>on the first day because he'd 
rather play with another pro.</i>

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:11.610
<i>And this time, there's no deal for a 
chance to play against Ike-kun.</i>

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:13.350
<i>There's no way he'd accept.</i>

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:14.790
Okay.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.290
<i>Huh?! He agreed?</i>

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.960
All right, since the tournament's coming up,

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:23.300
we'll make it a three-set match.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.050
<i>Three sets, even?</i>

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.580
<i>Does this mean he's starting to respect me?</i>

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:31.950
<i>Or is it because this is his last 
chance to play a match with me?</i>

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:35.640
<i>No, that's not what's important right now.</i>

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.640
<i>Only the results will determine 
who is worthy of facing Alex.</i>

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:43.600
<i>I learned how important results were 
the hard way during my two weeks here.</i>

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:45.030
<i>I want to win.</i>

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:49.240
Three-set match. Alex to serve.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:50.800
Which one?

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.960
Maruo's been feeling pretty 
confident since he beat you.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:55.990
Nah, it'll probably be Alex.

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:57.130
You never know.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.180
Maruo's gotten a lot better.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:05.880
<i>During these two weeks, I learned that when 
Alex serves from the deuce court,</i>

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:07.940
Wide 60% 
(Slice)

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.750
<i>he uses a wide slice 60% of the time.</i>

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.050
<i>The rest of his serves are flat 
with a spin and up the center.</i>

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:14.700
Center 30%
(Flat Spin)

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.850
<i>And after a spin, he tends to rush to the net.</i>

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:17.680
<i>I'll use this data and everything 
I've learned here...</i>

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:18.930
<i>to win!</i>

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:21.810
It's a spin up the center!

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:23.510
<i>I have to get to the ball 
while it's on the rise,</i>

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.070
<i>before Alex comes forward!</i>

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:25.690
<i>So heavy!</i>

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:27.080
He was able to stop it!

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.570
Alex hasn't made it to the net yet!

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:30.820
That was a weak hit!

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:32.790
<i>All right!</i>

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:34.290
Love-15.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:36.250
Whoa!

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:38.390
That's way different from 
how he played before!

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:40.580
That's why I said you never know.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.880
<i>Even from the ad court, 
he aims wide 60% of the time,</i>

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.700
<i>and up the center 30% of the time.</i>

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:49.590
<i>But unlike on the deuce court, 
I have to watch for the wide flat shot.</i>

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:52.420
Nice return!

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:53.590
But that wasn't enough.

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:56.970
<i>Cross-court is ideal for a forehand!</i>

00:15:57.940 --> 00:15:58.740
<i>Opposite?!</i>

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.250
Out. Love-30.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.120
Th-Thank goodness!

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.380
Aw, what a waste.

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:09.610
No, Maruo basically just forced him to miss.

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:16.900
<i>Right now, Alex is far more skilled than I am.</i>

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:19.120
<i>I'm no match for his technique, 
power, or experience.</i>

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:24.120
<i>But I can make up the gap between us 
by making use of what I've studied!</i>

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:27.130
<i>I will win, and I'll see what lies beyond!</i>

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:30.130
Yes!

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.130
Game: Maruo. 1-0.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:35.640
I can't believe he broke Alex's serve.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.890
Game: Maruo. 2-0.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:40.390
Game: Alex. 2-1.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.640
Game: Maruo. 3-1.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:44.890
Game: Alex. 3-2.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:50.200
I never would've expected this game to 
progress like this after Maruo's one-break lead.

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:59.370
<i>My pattern of going in with a forehand 
after my serve is obvious to him.</i>

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:01.910
<i>So this is the power of those notebooks?</i>

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:08.690
Game: Maruo. Maruo leads, 4-2.

00:17:11.620 --> 00:17:15.820
<i>Just two weeks ago, Maruo wasn't 
even worth a second look.</i>

00:17:15.820 --> 00:17:21.220
<i>And now, because of his meticulous 
reviewing, he's leading this game.</i>

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:27.690
Fault!

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.770
<i>A flat wide shot from the deuce court?</i>

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:33.190
<i>I've never seen that pattern 
in his serves before.</i>

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:36.570
<i>A slice up the middle!</i>

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:37.950
<i>This is new, too!</i>

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.230
<i>A slice backhand!</i>

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:43.700
<i>But he's been pivoting forward with 
a forehand or using a spin until now!</i>

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:46.400
<i>Oh, crap!</i>

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:49.540
15-love.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.550
<i>It's starting to deviate from my data...</i>

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:55.610
<i>I know exactly what's in those notebooks.</i>

00:17:56.270 --> 00:18:00.050
<i>It's my own past. I just didn't look back on it.</i>

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:05.550
<i>If you think you've figured me out in just 
two weeks, you're sorely mistaken.</i>

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:08.060
<i>I'll be using that notion against you.</i>

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.200
<i>This tennis differs from my data...</i>

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:14.840
<i>If Alex is changing his strategy, does 
that mean he caught on to my strategy?</i>

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.900
<i>Which means I broke through the first phase!</i>

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.290
<i>This proves that the tactics 
in my notebook worked!</i>

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:24.830
<i>I knew that Alex would change
his strategy if what I wrote worked.</i>

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:29.410
<i>In the second phase, he was focused 
on the opposite half of the court.</i>

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:33.300
<i>His ad court serves go toward the 
center now rather than wide.</i>

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:35.340
Wide

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:35.340
Center

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:36.290
<i>Look out for spins more than flats!</i>

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:40.170
<i>Before now, if it spun at the 
center, he'd dash forward.</i>

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.600
<i>But now, he's not coming forward!</i>

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.970
<i>Push forward while I can 
still read the situation!</i>

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:46.720
<i>Use my control to reach the back!</i>

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:49.560
<i>Return dash!</i>

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:51.600
Yes!

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.250
15-all.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:54.910
<i>He saw through me again.</i>

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.820
<i>How was he able to see that?</i>

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.460
<i>After he confirmed that 
I wasn't coming to the net,</i>

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:04.200
<i>he started hitting to my backhand.</i>

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.990
<i>He focused on volleying and didn't 
let me pivot for my forehand.</i>

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:12.960
<i>He steadied his delivery, and now 
he's trying to lock me in a stalemate.</i>

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:15.780
<i>Is he waiting for me to make a move first?</i>

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:17.960
<i>If that's the case,</i>

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:20.220
<i>I'll pivot, and hit up the line with my forehand!</i>

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:21.570
<i>There it is!</i>

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:27.560
15-30.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:28.810
Yes!

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.220
That's amazing.

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:32.600
He met that forehand with a return ace.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.810
He read Alex's moves.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:36.750
<i>I know now.</i>

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:38.250
<i>It's not about technique.</i>

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.520
<i>His strategy is a step ahead of mine.</i>

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.170
<i>Does this mean he's 
gone so far as to envision</i>

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.380
<i>what it's like to have these returned?</i>

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:51.640
<i>That he knows what patterns to expect 
next if I change my strategy?</i>

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.030
<i>If he's writing not just from the past,</i>

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:55.830
<i>but also what could happen in the future,</i>

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.130
<i>I've underestimated those notebooks.</i>

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.840
<i>I finally understand what 
Soji was talking about.</i>

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.850
But he probably won't let you win so easily.

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.800
<i>That may very well be the case.</i>

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:12.110
<i>But...</i>

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:13.850
<i>I will win this match!</i>

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:17.480
Out!

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:18.860
15-40!

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.390
Game: Maruo. 5-2.

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:24.520
Whoa!

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:27.120
He broke Alex's serve again!

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.120
You prevailed, didn't you?

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.010
What is there in your past for you to regret?

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:39.630
Stop looking back, believe in 
yourself, and face forward!

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:43.690
<i>Dad, I've played offensively 
while only looking forward,</i>

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.810
<i>never fearing mistakes or looking back.</i>

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:49.410
<i>I've gotten this far because 
I was able to do so.</i>

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:54.140
<i>But I'm missing something 
necessary to move forward...</i>

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:56.160
<i>Even if I can't change the past,</i>

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.190
<i>if there's something there 
that can change the future...</i>

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:00.790
Time!

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.580
<i>Maybe there are pasts that are</i>

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.600
<i>meant to be forgotten, 
and others meant to be accepted.</i>

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:07.910
15-love.

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:10.660
Out. 30-love.

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.850
Alex is starting to make more errors now.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:14.540
Yeah...

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:17.310
He's not only attacking with his forehand,

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.040
he's starting to use his backhand, too.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:22.140
Normally you'd never see someone 
on his level do such a thing.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:23.510
30-15.

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.800
Like changing his basic style 
in the middle of a match.

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:28.600
That's probably why he's missing so much.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:29.300
Out. 40-15.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:36.190
Out!

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:39.060
First set winner: Maruo.

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:40.820
Score: 6-2.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:42.240
I can't believe it!

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:43.920
Maruo won a set!

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:46.770
He would've won the whole thing 
if it weren't a three-set match!

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:51.330
No, I think Alex is knowingly 
playing this as a three-set match.

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.680
<i>All right. That was worth one ball.</i>

00:21:56.250 --> 00:22:00.080
<i>It's not that my backhand is bad.
I'm just confident in my forehand.</i>

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:02.580
<i>If you're coming at me with data,</i>

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.200
<i>I'll just have to play in a manner 
that you can't figure out.</i>

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:08.510
<i>It'll take a bit of time to 
get used to this style of play,</i>

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.090
<i>but I can adjust it.</i>

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:13.190
<i>Your notebook was impressive.</i>

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:18.730
<i>Those strong backhands will 
eventually score a point.</i>

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:22.110
<i>If that happens, I won't be able 
to use my strategy from before.</i>

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:27.090
<i>This is where...</i>

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:31.420
<i>This is where the real match starts!</i>

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:25.020
Next Time

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.330
His play style is different now.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:03.740
I have to find a way to deal

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:05.790
with the different serves 
without relying on my data.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:10.030
Isn't there some way to narrow 
down all the possibilities?

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.570
I can't think of anything!

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:15.430
If that's the case, I'll use a simpler strategy.

00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:17.090
I won't let him use his forehand so easily!

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:18.240
That's it!

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:20.980
Watch carefully and go for it!

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:22.730
Next time on <i>Baby Steps</i>,

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:25.020
Awakening to Simplicity

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:24.020
"Awakening to Simplicity."

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:30.340
End
